# If you prefer the allow list template instead of the deny list, see community template:
# https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/community/Golang/Go.AllowList.gitignore
#
# Binaries for programs and plugins
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

# Test binary, built with `go test -c`
*.test

# Output of the go coverage tool, specifically when used with LiteIDE
*.out

# Dependency directories (remove the comment below to include it)
# vendor/

# Go workspace file
go.work

# macOS
.DS_Store

# The compiled CLI, when built in place with `go build ./cmd/flow`.
/flow

# The compiled gate, for the same reason: `go build ./tools/gate` writes it
# here, and `git add -A` after a gate run then commits five megabytes of
# machine-specific binary. Which is exactly what happened on the branch that
# added this line.
/gate

# `go build ./tools/vacuity` writes the binary here, and a `git add -A` after it
# committed 3.3 MB of x86-64 ELF to this repository (#1125, caught by Codex
# after it had already merged). Nothing consumes it — `make vacuity` runs
# `go run ./tools/vacuity` — so the entry is the whole fix, beside the ones
# above for exactly the same reason.
/vacuity

# The embedding example's binary, for the same reason: `go build
# ./examples/embedding` from the repo root writes it here. A 46 MB copy was
# committed exactly this way in ff6131b and removed by #922.
/embedding

# Plugin binaries, when built in place per a plugin's own README
# (`go build -o .../flowstate-plugin-<name> ./plugins/<name>`) rather than
# into a directory already outside the tree.
/plugins/*/flowstate-plugin-*
/plugins/*/bin/

# Agent worktrees. Worktree isolation for subagents materializes under
# .claude/worktrees/, which is scratch by definition: each is a copy of the
# tree mid-edit, and committing one would commit a snapshot of unfinished
# work twice over. Everything else under .claude/ (skills, commands) is
# checked in.
.claude/worktrees/

.worktrees/

# `make coverage`'s output (#519): raw GOCOVERDIR counters, the merged
# profile, the HTML report and the per-package summary. Generated fresh by
# every run and never a statement about a specific commit worth committing.
.coverage/
